WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.671
Picture the loading dock behind our post office.

2
00:00:05.671 --> 00:00:10.016
A van pulls up for a sack of parcels that's already packed and labeled.

3
00:00:10.016 --> 00:00:15.218
Nobody carries that sack to the counter, unpacks it, repacks it, and carries it back.

4
00:00:15.218 --> 00:00:17.544
The dock hands it straight to the van.

5
00:00:17.733 --> 00:00:20.542
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

6
00:00:20.542 --> 00:00:24.693
Sending a file over the network is just read, then write, in a loop.

7
00:00:24.693 --> 00:00:26.525
We've all written it that way.

8
00:00:26.525 --> 00:00:28.661
So what could be wasteful about it?

9
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.013
Let's look at what that loop really does.

10
00:00:32.013 --> 00:00:35.328
A socket is a program's end of a network connection.

11
00:00:35.328 --> 00:00:42.084
The kernel, the core of the operating system, keeps recently used file pages in memory, in its page cache.

12
00:00:42.084 --> 00:00:43.805
Those are our packed sacks.

13
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.331
Your loop calls read.

14
00:00:45.331 --> 00:00:50.021
That's a system call: a switch from your program into the kernel and back.

15
00:00:50.021 --> 00:00:53.696
The kernel copies a chunk of the file up into your buffer.

16
00:00:53.696 --> 00:00:54.267
Copy one.

17
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:55.748
Then you call write.

18
00:00:55.748 --> 00:01:02.423
Another system call, and the kernel copies the same bytes back down, into the socket's send buffer.

19
00:01:02.423 --> 00:01:03.030
Copy two.

20
00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:05.861
Only then does the network card send them.

21
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:13.044
With a heap ByteBuffer, the JDK even adds another copy, through a temporary buffer.

22
00:01:13.044 --> 00:01:18.984
And a 64 MiB file through a 64 KiB buffer is 1,024 rounds of all this.

23
00:01:19.133 --> 00:01:28.053
Every byte takes a round trip through your program, like a traveler who flies to a city only to change planes, and never sees the view.

24
00:01:28.053 --> 00:01:30.630
Your program never changes these bytes.

25
00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:32.017
It only carries them.

26
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:37.199
Wouldn't it be nice to tell the kernel: you have the file, you have the socket, you move it?

27
00:01:37.333 --> 00:01:40.517
That's exactly what FileChannel.transferTo does.

28
00:01:40.517 --> 00:01:46.288
On Linux, with a socket as the target, the JDK turns it into one system call, sendfile.

29
00:01:46.288 --> 00:01:53.320
The kernel sends pages from the page cache straight to the socket, and the bytes never enter your process.

30
00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:57.499
That's called zero-copy: no copy through your program's memory.

31
00:01:57.667 --> 00:01:58.490
Let's map it.

32
00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:01.277
The page cache is the stack of packed sacks.

33
00:02:01.277 --> 00:02:03.557
The socket's send buffer is the van.

34
00:02:03.557 --> 00:02:09.384
Your program is the counter, and with zero-copy, the sack never comes to the counter at all.

35
00:02:10.133 --> 00:02:19.992
The lesson's ZeroCopyDemo sends a 64 MiB file both ways, and strace, a Linux tool that lists every system call, counted them.

36
00:02:19.992 --> 00:02:21.885
How many calls for each?

37
00:02:21.885 --> 00:02:22.911
Make a guess.

38
00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:28.452
transferTo: one sendfile call.

39
00:02:28.452 --> 00:02:36.152
The copy: 2,119 reads and 1,037 writes, and the reads include the receiving side.

40
00:02:36.333 --> 00:02:38.952
About three thousand calls against one.

41
00:02:38.952 --> 00:02:41.637
So zero-copy must be much faster, right?

42
00:02:43.867 --> 00:02:44.291
Well.

43
00:02:44.291 --> 00:02:51.161
The two passes took 78 and 74 milliseconds in one run, and 56 and 38 in the next.

44
00:02:51.161 --> 00:02:54.553
Close, and not the same way round twice.

45
00:02:54.733 --> 00:02:55.615
Why so close?

46
00:02:55.615 --> 00:03:03.414
The file was already in the page cache, and the socket went from the machine to itself, so copying 64 MiB is quick.

47
00:03:03.414 --> 00:03:05.923
One timing proves nothing either way.

48
00:03:05.923 --> 00:03:11.891
The honest measure here is the system calls: they count the work that zero-copy removes.

49
00:03:12.667 --> 00:03:13.625
Two guardrails.

50
00:03:13.625 --> 00:03:17.329
First, transferTo may move fewer bytes than you asked for.

51
00:03:17.329 --> 00:03:23.076
It returns how many it moved, so call it in a loop, just like a partial write in lesson 2.

52
00:03:23.267 --> 00:03:27.555
Second, the bytes must leave exactly as they sit in the file.

53
00:03:27.555 --> 00:03:36.624
If the JVM encrypts them with TLS, the encryption behind https, or compresses them, they have to come up into your memory anyway.

54
00:03:36.624 --> 00:03:41.335
A sack that must be opened on the way can't go straight to the van.

55
00:03:41.533 --> 00:03:50.671
Tomcat, a Java web server, shows the trade-off: for static files over 48 KiB, it uses sendfile and skips compression.

56
00:03:51.467 --> 00:03:53.230
Zero-copy saves the copies.

57
00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:57.933
The next question is how a server learns which sockets are ready at all.

58
00:03:57.933 --> 00:04:02.570
Everything in this track so far used epoll, Linux's board of lit boxes.

59
00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:07.273
You register each socket once, and epoll_wait returns only the lit ones.

60
00:04:07.273 --> 00:04:10.081
Then your loop makes each read call itself.

61
00:04:10.267 --> 00:04:11.820
That's called readiness.

62
00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.187
The kernel says: this box has mail, come and get it.

63
00:04:15.933 --> 00:04:20.479
io_uring is a newer Linux interface, and it works by completion.

64
00:04:20.479 --> 00:04:26.588
Your program and the kernel share two rings in memory, like a drop box with two trays.

65
00:04:26.588 --> 00:04:31.276
You drop requests in one tray: read up to 16 KiB from this socket.

66
00:04:31.276 --> 00:04:36.888
The kernel does the work and puts results in the other tray: done, 4,096 bytes.

67
00:04:37.067 --> 00:04:43.823
One system call can hand over and collect many jobs, and the kernel, not your thread, moves the bytes.

68
00:04:43.823 --> 00:04:49.453
On the JVM you reach it through Netty, where it's a full transport since version 4.2.

69
00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:53.193
So the new drop box must be faster, right?

70
00:04:53.193 --> 00:04:58.609
The lesson's TransportsDemo sent 32 MiB over each transport on this machine.

71
00:04:58.609 --> 00:04:59.820
Guess the winner.

72
00:05:02.533 --> 00:05:08.977
Every transport delivered all 33,554,432 bytes.

73
00:05:08.977 --> 00:05:16.243
nio took 137 milliseconds, epoll 35, and io_uring 59.

74
00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:17.833
And not just once.

75
00:05:17.833 --> 00:05:23.965
The Kotlin twin measured 40 against 48, and the research runs 86 against 159.

76
00:05:23.965 --> 00:05:28.345
The gaps moved from run to run, and epoll kept winning.

77
00:05:28.533 --> 00:05:30.312
Newer is a date, not a speed.

78
00:05:30.312 --> 00:05:34.913
It tells you when something was made, not how fast it runs on your machine.

79
00:05:34.913 --> 00:05:37.796
So measure your own workload before you switch.

80
00:05:37.796 --> 00:05:42.704
The switch is one factory and one channel class, cheap to try and cheap to undo.

81
00:05:43.467 --> 00:05:46.851
Here's the zero-copy loop in Java and in Kotlin.

82
00:05:46.851 --> 00:05:50.307
Keep calling transferTo until every byte is sent.

83
00:05:51.067 --> 00:05:53.651
So, what was wasteful about read and write?

84
00:05:53.651 --> 00:05:56.656
Every byte made a round trip through your program.

85
00:05:56.656 --> 00:06:02.245
When bytes leave exactly as they're stored, let the kernel move them, dock to van, in a loop.

86
00:06:02.245 --> 00:06:06.091
And treat a newer kernel interface as a question, not an answer.

87
00:06:06.267 --> 00:06:07.119
Quick check.

88
00:06:07.119 --> 00:06:10.740
Your server compresses each file before sending it.

89
00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:12.303
Can it use transferTo?

90
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:15.219
No.

91
00:06:15.219 --> 00:06:22.096
Compression changes the bytes inside the JVM, so they have to come up into your memory anyway.
